  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-2,3,1)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$r=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n$3x\\,-\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$y\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$z\\,+$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$=0$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=\u27e8-2,3,1\u27e9.$  From the vectorial equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $\u27e83,-5,2\u27e9.$  So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=\u27e83,-5,2\u27e9.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot \u27e83,-5,2\u27e9 & =\u27e8-2,3,1\u27e9\\cdot \u27e83,-5,2\u27e9 \\\\ r\\cdot \u27e83,-5,2\u27e9 & =-19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=\u27e8x,y,z\u27e9$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u27e8x,y,z\u27e9\\cdot \u27e83,-5,2\u27e9 & =-19 \\\\ 3x-5y+2z & =-19 \\\\ 3x-5y+2z+19 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
